<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-function-notation">
  <title>Function Notation: Graphs, Tables, and Formulas</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Today's activity is about function notation <m>f(c)</m>: the name of a function <m>f</m>, followed by a specific input value <m>c</m> in parentheses. It is tempting to read this the way parentheses next to a letter in algebra usually imply multiplication, "<m>f</m> times <m>c</m>." That is <em>not</em> what is happening here. <m>f(c)</m> is a single symbol. All by itself, it names one number: the output that the function <m>f</m> produces when the input is <m>c</m>. Instead we read, "<m>f</m> of <m>c</m>" or "the value of <m>f</m> at <m>c</m>."
    </p>
    <p>
      You will meet functions described three different ways today: as a graph, as a table, and as a formula. You will practice reading <m>f(c)</m> the same way no matter which description you're given.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-function-notation-graph" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 1: Reading Values from a Graph</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Every time you evaluate a function, think of it like a card representing a value: the front reads something like <m>f(-2)</m>, and the back reads whatever number that turns out to be. Evaluating a function is <q>flipping the card over</q>. The front and the back are two views of the very same value and are interchangeable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here is the graph of a function <m>f</m>.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-function-notation-graph">
        <caption>The graph of <m>f</m></caption>
        <image xml:id="image-function-notation-graph" width="60%">
          <shortdescription>
            A piecewise graph with three pieces. A rising line segment runs from a closed point
            at (-4,1) up to an open point at (-2,3). A upward-curving parabola arc runs from a
            closed point at (-2,4) to a closed point at (1,1), with an open point (a hole) at
            (-0.5, 0.25) where the curve is interrupted. A falling line segment runs from an open
            point at (1,3) down to a closed point at (4,0).
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9, >=stealth]
              % axes
              \draw[->] (-4.6,0) -- (4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,5.2) node[above] {$y$};
              % tick marks
              \foreach \x in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4} {
                \draw (\x,0.08) -- (\x,-0.08);
                \node[below] at (\x,-0.08) {\small $\x$};
              }
              \foreach \y in {1,2,3,4,5} {
                \draw (0.08,\y) -- (-0.08,\y);
                \node[left] at (-0.08,\y) {\small $\y$};
              }

              % Piece 1: f(x) = x+5 on [-4,-2)
              \draw[thick, domain=-4:-2, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x+5});
              \filldraw (-4,1) circle (2.2pt);
              \draw[fill=white] (-2,3) circle (2.2pt);

              % Piece 2: f(x) = x^2 on [-2,1], hole at x=-0.5
              \draw[thick, domain=-2:-0.55, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x*\x});
              \draw[thick, domain=-0.45:1, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x*\x});
              \filldraw (-2,4) circle (2.2pt);
              \draw[fill=white] (-0.5,0.25) circle (2.2pt);
              \filldraw (1,1) circle (2.2pt);

              % Piece 3: f(x) = -x+4 on (1,4]
              \draw[thick, domain=1:4, samples=50] plot (\x, {-\x+4});
              \draw[fill=white] (1,3) circle (2.2pt);
              \filldraw (4,0) circle (2.2pt);
            \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        A filled-in point is part of the graph of <m>f</m>; an open point is <em>not</em> part of the graph, but illustrates that the curve would reach it if it were closed. Most <m>x</m>-values on this graph don't have any special point marked at all and are just read off where the curve passes through.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-graph-jump-left" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>f(-2)</m>. Make a card for it.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-graph-jump-right" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>f(1)</m>. Make a card for it.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-graph-hole" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>f(-0.5)</m>. Make a card for it. (If you don't think this has a value, say so on the back of the card, and be ready to explain why.)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-graph-ordinary-point" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>f(-1)</m>. Make a card for it.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-function-notation-table" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 2: Reading Values from a Table, and Combining Values</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Here is a table for a second function, <m>g</m>. To find <m>g(c)</m>, look through the top row for the input value <m>c</m>, then read the number underneath it.
      </p>
      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell><m>x</m></cell>
          <cell>-3</cell>
          <cell>-1</cell>
          <cell>0</cell>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell>3</cell>
          <cell>4</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
          <cell>5</cell>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell>-1</cell>
          <cell>0</cell>
          <cell>4</cell>
          <cell>1</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
      <p>
        Making a card for a value read from a table works exactly the same way as it did for the graph. The input value is found in the <m>x</m> row. The value of the function (back of the card) is found in the <m>g(x)</m> row.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some expressions below use more than one function evaluation. In those cases, make a card for <em>each</em> evaluation first. Then use the compound expression starting with the function side up and then flip each card over. Use the new representation showing the values to complete the arithmetic.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-table-product" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the value of <m>g(0) \cdot f(-1)</m>. Make a card for <m>g(0)</m> and a card for <m>f(-1)</m> first, then multiply the two numbers on their backs.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-table-compound" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the value of <m>\bigl(2f(-2) - 1\bigr)^2</m>. Make a card for <m>f(-2)</m> first.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-function-notation-formula" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 3: Formulas as Evaluation Rules</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A formula is a third way to describe a function. Instead of reading a picture or a table, you calculate the <q>number on the back</q> using a rule described by the formula. Although a formula is usually given using a variable like <m>x</m>
        <md>
          h(x) = \begin{cases}
          x^2 - 1, \amp x \lt 1 \\
          1 - x, \amp\ x \ge 1
          \end{cases}
        </md>,
        it can help to write the rule with a box in place of the input, like this:
        <md>
          h(\Box) = \begin{cases}
          (\Box)^2 - 1, \amp \Box \lt 1 \\
          1 - (\Box), \amp\ \Box \ge 1
          \end{cases}
        </md>
        Whatever number goes inside the box in the parentheses of <m>h(\Box)</m>,
        that same number must go in every box on the other side with the rule.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a <term>piecewise</term> formula: <m>h</m> has two different rules, and each rule is associated with a different interval described by an inequality.
        Which one you use depends on where the input falls. To evaluate <m>h(c)</m>, first decide which condition <m>c</m> satisfies (<m>c \lt 1</m> or <m>c \ge 1</m>) and only then fill that rule's formula box with <m>c</m> everywhere it appears.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-formula-piece-one" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>h(-2)</m>. Make a card for it.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-formula-piece-two" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>h(2)</m>. Make a card for it.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-formula-boundary" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>h(1)</m>. Make a card for it. Which rule did you use, and why?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-function-notation-together" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 4: Putting It Together</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        You now have three functions on the table: <m>f</m> from its graph, <m>g</m> from its table, and <m>h</m> from its piecewise formula. They're all the same kind of object. Each one hands you a single output value for a given input. Make a card for every evaluation before combining values.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-together-one" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the value of <m>g(-3) - 2h(-2) + f(1)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-together-two" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the value of <m>\bigl(g(3) - h(1)\bigr)^2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
